{
 "id": "future-passive",
 "title": "Future Passive Indicative",
 "course": "Verbs · Passive Indicative",
 "subtitle": "Flashcards for all four conjugations plus 3rd -iō",
 "intro": "The passive uses the same stems and time markers as the active. Only the personal endings change: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span> replace <span class=\"end\">-ō/-m, -s, -t, -mus, -tis, -nt</span>.<p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\">The future keeps its <b>two patterns</b>:</p><ul><li><b>1st and 2nd (-bi- future):</b> <span class=\"end\">-bor, -beris, -bitur, -bimur, -biminī, -buntur</span>. Note the short <b>e</b> in amāb<b>e</b>ris.</li><li><b>3rd, 3rd -iō and 4th (vowel shift):</b> <span class=\"end\">-ar, -ēris, -ētur, -ēmur, -ēminī, -entur</span> (mittar, audiar).</li></ul><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch out:</b> mittēris (future, “you will be sent”) and mitteris (present, “you are sent”) differ only in vowel length.</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Passive endings: <span class=\"end\">-r, -ris, -tur, -mur, -minī, -ntur</span>",
